**EXERCISE 4.17.** (For algebraists). Suppose $\langle S, 1, \cdot \rangle$ is a semi-group with unit (sometimes called a monoid). Remark that $PS$ is a domain. For $a, b \in S$, what is the least $x \in PS$ such that

$$
x = \{1\} \cup \{a, b\} \cup x \cdot x,
$$

where in general for $x, y \subseteq S$

$$
x \cdot y = \{ t \cdot u \mid t \in x \text{ and } u \in y \}?
$$

Need the fixed point be unique?

<!-- page 70 -->

The powerset operator, non-uniqueness of fixed points, and identification of the least solution with the submonoid generated by $\{a,b\}$ are with the
[Generated submonoid](1980_genSubmonoid.md) peer.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`example_417`).** On $\mathbb{N}$, the operator $F\,1\,1$ has distinct fixed points $\{1\}$ and $\mathbb{N}$; for any monoid, the least fixed set of $F\,a\,b$ equals the submonoid closure of $\{a,b\}$.

*Proof.* Exhibit the two fixed points on $\mathbb{N}$, then invoke the closure identification for general $a,b$. ∎
